```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Current Time</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Current Time on the East Coast</h1>
    <div id="clock"></div>
    <script>
        function updateClock() {
            const now = new Date();
            const timeZone = "America/New_York"; // America/New_York handles DST automatically
            const options = { 
                timeZone: timeZone, 
                hour: '2-digit', 
                minute: '2-digit', 
                second: '2-digit',
                hour12: false 
            };
            const timeString = now.toLocaleTimeString('en-US', options);
            document.getElementById('clock').textContent = timeString;
        }
        updateClock();
        setInterval(updateClock, 1000);
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Assumptions:** This HTML/JavaScript code runs in any modern browser. It uses the browser's built-in `Intl.DateTimeFormat` API (or equivalent logic via `toLocaleTimeString` with a timezone string) to calculate the time in the `America/New_York` timezone, which automatically accounts for Daylight Saving Time.
